xports its types, services, and commands\n2. `lib.rs` (StackPilot shell) does:\n   ```rust\n   mod modules {\n       pub mod devlauncher;\n       pub mod workspace;\n   }\n   ```\n3. `AppState` in `core/app_state.rs` contains references to all module services:\n   ```rust\n   pub struct StackPilotAppState {\n       pub devlauncher: devlauncher::DevLauncherState,\n       pub workspace: workspace::WorkspaceState,\n       pub settings: Arc<dyn SettingsService>,\n   }\n   ```\n4. Each module's `mod.rs` defines its own state struct:\n   ```rust\n   pub mod devlauncher {\n       pub struct DevLauncherState {\n           pub profile_manager: Arc<dyn ProfileManager>,\n           pub launch_engine: Arc<dyn LaunchEngine>,\n           pub analyzer: Arc<dyn ProjectAnalyzer>,\n       }\n       \n       pub mod commands {\n           // Tauri commands specific to DevLauncher\n           // Each command takes State<StackPilotAppState> and accesses state.devlauncher.*\n       }\n   }\n   ```\n5. Commands are still registered in `lib.rs`:\n   ```rust\n   .invoke_handler(tauri::generate_handler![\n       modules::devlauncher::commands::list_profiles,\n       modules::workspace::commands::list_processes,\n       ...\n   ])\n   ```\n\nBut wait - Tauri commands need to take `State<'_, AppState>` where `AppState` is the STATE type. If commands are in separate modules, they still need to know about `StackPilotAppState` which includes ALL modules. This creates a dependency: `devlauncher::commands` needs to know about `StackPilotAppState` which is defined in `core::app_state`. This is fine - commands are thin wrappers, the business logic stays in the module.\n\nActually, there's a cleaner approach. We can use Tauri's state management more elegantly:\n\n```rust\n// core/app_state.rs\npub struct DevLauncherState {\n    pub profile_manager: Arc<dyn ProfileManager>,\n    pub launch_engine: Arc<dyn LaunchEngine>,\n    pub analyzer: Arc<dyn ProjectAnalyzer>,\n}\n\npub struct WorkspaceState {\n    pub process_manager: Arc<dyn ProcessManager>,\n}\n\n// lib.rs - register separate states\napp.manage(DevLauncherState { ... });\napp.manage(WorkspaceState { ... });\napp.manage(SettingsState { ... });\n\n// commands in devlauncher module access DevLauncherState directly\n#[tauri::command]\npub fn list_profiles(state: State<'_, DevLauncherState>) -> ... {\n    state.profile_manager.list_profiles()\n}\n```\n\nThis is much cleaner! Each module's commands only need to know about their own State type. No circular dependencies.\n\nBut wait - there's a Tauri limitation. `State<'_, T>` requires `T: Send + Sync + 'static`. Each state type must be unique. Since we're using separate structs, this works.\n\nFor the frontend, since we're using SvelteKit with file-based routing, each module's routes are naturally separated. The `+layout.svelte` at the root provides the StackPilot shell (navigation, global styles). Each module's pages are in their own route folder.\n\nThe API layer (`api.ts`) currently centralizes all invoke calls. For modularity, we could split this:\n- `src/core/api.ts` - core API (ping, settings)\n- `src/modules/devlauncher/api.ts` - DevLauncher API calls\n- `src/modules/workspace/api.ts` - Workspace API calls\n\nBut for SvelteKit routes, importing from `src/modules/devlauncher/api.ts` is a bit of a path traversal. We could use SvelteKit's `$lib` alias:\n- `$lib/core/api.ts`\n- `$lib/modules/devlauncher/api.ts`\n\nOr simpler: keep a flat structure but namespace clearly:\n- `$lib/api/core.ts`\n- `$lib/api/devlauncher.ts`\n- `$lib/api/workspace.ts`\n\nOK, let me now present this architectural analysis clearly to the user.\n\nFor the **migration plan**, I need a step-by-step approach:\n\n1. Create the new directory structure\n2. Move models.rs to core/\n3. Move settings to core/\n4. Move DevLauncher files to modules/devlauncher/\n5. Move process_manager to modules/workspace/\n6. Update all imports and paths\n7. Update lib.rs to use new module structure\n8. Update